#{[17592186158129 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158129 #edb/ref 1061 26397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158129 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ef8179b-8279-55d0-b781-5afd8ba23248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158129 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158474 #edb/ref 1064 "The **difference** of two sets $A$ and $B,$ denoted by $A \\setminus B$ or $A - B,$ is the set of all elements belonging to $A$ and *not* belonging to $B.$\n\nFor example, consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4a2d3be8c64b5527dcb37ac68d99cd1e36009725d0847597c5bbd417bbd5f7ad.png)\n\nThe difference $A \\setminus B$ consists of all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$ To represent $A \\setminus B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained within $A$ but not $B.$\n\n![](images/49/49743d4647817294c2287bc15a496fb5d6c73070ed52cfd8934d1babe93c1b25.png)\n\nThe elements $a$ and $c$ are contained in $A$ but not $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The difference can be defined using set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ x \\mid x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B \\}\n$$\n\nThe condition \"$x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B$\" means that $x$ must belong to $A$ but $x$ must *not* belong to $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158474 #edb/ref 1065 4333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48529a2b-cc59-57e8-8624-78fd4339f88c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158474 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158475 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a set is a collection of objects. The set containing *all* objects that we can consider is called the **universal set**.\n\nFor example, if the universal set is $U = \\{ a, b, c, d \\},$ then the objects that we can consider are the letters $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n- One set we can consider is $\\{ a, b, d \\}.$ We consider this set because all of its elements are taken from the universal set. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- One that we *cannot* consider is $\\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}.$ We do *not* consider this set because its elements are *not* taken from the universal set. $\\quad\\color{red}\\times$\n\n- Likewise, we cannot consider the set $\\{ c, d, e\\}$ because one of its elements, $e,$ is *not* taken from the universal set. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158475 #edb/ref 1065 4334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158475 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "08cce8fa-a37d-53dd-a7fa-3aae49e7a4a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158475 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158476 #edb/ref 1064 "If $S$ is a set in the universe $U,$ then the **complement** of $S,$ denoted $\\overline{S},$ consists of every element in $U$ that is **not** in the set $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $S = \\{ 2, 5\\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}.$ We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/20/20726b35619c1b04803c18c8fe12f52db8f07faebca45f64a86c17f8f7077d09.png)\n\nThe complement of $S,$ denoted $\\overline S,$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $S.$ To represent $\\overline S,$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $S,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c7/c7b25f22a1f70135d09886db58be3646604a861e62d34978a3563343fcd7b298.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $1,$ $3,$ and $4.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $S.$ Therefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{S} = \\{ 1,3,4 \\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that there are several ways to denote the complement of a set. The complement of a set $S$ can be denoted as $\\overline{S},$ or $S^c,$ or $S'.$ Here, we will use the convention $\\overline{S}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158476 #edb/ref 1065 4336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e80b18cd-6881-563b-a21e-aca78742ce9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158476 #edb/ref 1067 "The Complement of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158477 #edb/ref 1064 "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize complements, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a complement.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the set $S = \\{ 2, 5\\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}.$ This time, we will compute the complement $\\overline S$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements of $U$ that are not in $S.$\n\nSo, we go through the elements of $U$ and check if they are in $S,$ as follows. Remember that we are looking for elements that are *not* in $S.$\n\n- For the element $1 \\in U,$ we have $1 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $2 \\in U,$ we have $2 \\in S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $3 \\in U,$ we have $3 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $4 \\in U,$ we have $4 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $5 \\in U,$ we have $5 \\in S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nWe found that the elements $1,3,4$ are in $U$ but not $S.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{S} = \\{ 1,3,4 \\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158477 #edb/ref 1065 4337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158477 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6966558b-99a7-527c-b2bd-fe2f9b3a7d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158477 #edb/ref 1067 "Complements Without Venn Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158480 #edb/ref 1064 "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize differences, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a difference.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the difference $A \\setminus B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$\n\nSo, we will go through the elements of $A$ and check if each element is also in $B.$ Remember that we are looking for elements that are *not* in $B.$\n\n- For the element $a \\in A,$ we have $a \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $b \\in A,$ we have $b \\in B. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $c \\in A,$ we have $c \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nThe elements of $A$ that are not in $B$ are $a$ and $c.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158480 #edb/ref 1065 4342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158480 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "14afef0e-fec6-5cdf-82c4-98391973df49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158480 #edb/ref 1067 "Differences Without Venn Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Value of a Binomial Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171956 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "be11193b-a241-5812-a1c1-54d233ae9ddd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171957 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Binomial Coefficients Using the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171958 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5c108c0-f32a-5725-af3b-c3e747da09f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1045 "Pascal's Triangle and the Binomial Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5ec40baf-865b-5a44-991d-3e7fe88c0d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})=[]+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1afc9131-fb54-545a-abcd-cbf497ee2da8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1074 "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1061 18724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2865273f-2c72-526a-8ae4-5f9b2067a41a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1061 18731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1189fea7-65d2-5d2d-87d5-800d8204673c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1061 18977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "133af8ac-b2ce-5603-9334-ce3da1e8d068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1061 19269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b7576cb-372f-5a6a-ad5b-4f0b958a39e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1061 23007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2f96998-bbd7-586b-b0d8-4cdbe2c42034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (on the left) is position number $0.$\n\nWe can write a value in Pascal's triangle as $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}},$ where the value is located in row $\\color{red}a$ and position ${\\color{blue}b}.$\nThis is known as a **binomial coefficient**.\nIn words, we say $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}}$ as \"$\\color{red}{a}$ choose ${\\color{blue}b}$\".\n\nFor example, the value located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{{\\color{red}4} \\choose \\color{blue}{2}} = 6.$ Remember, we start counting from zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1065 3393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "62d4b142-a86a-52c9-b45a-1153afedf865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we wanted to calculate the value of $\\displaystyle{30 \\choose 3}.$ If we were to use Pascal's triangle, we'd have to write out $31$ rows, which would take a long time. Thankfully, there is a formula for calculating binomial coefficients. \n\nThe binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1065 3893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b89d1a7e-10c1-5ec2-b745-c3c45e0d7814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1067 "A Formula for the Binomial Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237670 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3fb0c0d-1d27-592b-96c1-d094c4cc9822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95f7caf5956ab22fe5b47d14098730e23332c41a26d58fc8dc0f0e4ca28546ab.png)\n\nLet $U=\\{l,m,n,o\\}$ be the universal set and let $K=\\{l,m,o\\}.$ What is $\\overset{―}{K}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a339fd3-f450-5a9a-b0dd-ff8dba5b4293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237878 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $K,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{K},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $K.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{K},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $K,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74345aeb2a1028e07d155dd357a9bb412860309c99cee37c62380907b3e1bd37.png)\n\nThe element in the shaded area is $n.$ This is the only element of $U$ that is not in $K.$ Therefore, the complement of $K$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{K}=\\{n\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240345 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0c294f44-fff5-5b15-a966-35cefb0a5204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240596 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "969e0b9d-d3d2-5f29-a98a-b20b18e9c6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1071 "If $U=\\{2,4,6,8,…,20\\}$ is the universal set and $B=\\{4,8,12,16,20\\},$ then the complement of $B$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32ae7bbc-249c-5c79-aba5-9c3291b509c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240999 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $2,$ $6,$ $10,$ $14,$ and $18.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{B}=\\{2,6,10,14,18\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $U=\\{a,b,c,…,z\\}$ be the universal set and $Y=\\{a,b,c,…,j\\}$, then $\\overset{―}{Y}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aa7531f-69fb-514d-bcea-7f5e2dae1a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241005 #edb/ref 1074 "The complement of $Y,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Y},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $Y.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the letters $a$ through $z,$ while the set $Y$ consists of the letters $a$ through $j.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $Y$ are the letters $k$ through $z.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Y}=\\{k,l,m,…,z\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246939 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dafab0b3-0ea9-52f1-b23d-e401a912ba3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})=[]+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7e46def-b53d-544e-a445-f2ab7d8b4fbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247383 #edb/ref 1074 "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $6$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}) \\\\  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6}) \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250707 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [7,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250707 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250707 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11e5043a-3f1b-5f5b-bcbc-e166efd4ebcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251657 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in [-7,4]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251657 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251657 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "82e9a537-35f9-5e3b-ab01-9a35c8a25c58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251907 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in [-5,7]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ce64a4f-ea12-5a34-9745-a6ee77838e83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252329 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [7,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8e3439e-fc9c-56a7-abef-e1dbb6bcab4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252353 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [4,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb2e009d-1001-5e69-8ef1-819444c324cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252925 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4480b1db-53d1-58b7-9c5c-51fba18c5921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6eea42f5-3c3e-56b9-8e85-3dd91f7a6a1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94357139-827d-5170-82dc-cc21ec831009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7)\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f270ff5-bc17-53a5-be2e-3042a3d54393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,7),\\,(7,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup \\{-2\\}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a117df1a-bd81-5da6-88f9-198bb5436e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup \\{0\\}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A=\\{x,y,z\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dc7abe5-6826-51d9-82bd-d471faabcb9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/1d/1d7c11586a8fff5f63d46d936fd32c7b5fbfc33a406ddf268bfdd934fe787cdf.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{x\\},\\{y\\},\\{z\\},\\{x,y\\},\\{x,z\\},\\{y,z\\},\\{x,y,z\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1080 "∅" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "697eb32b-6e7b-5b33-b678-ff2fa69f0dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3fdd8f7f-a798-5a0a-b0d9-7c92641e5e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256119 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69b8c829-6ae3-5595-acab-b091ccf66bf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1071 "If $X=\\{1,3,5,7\\}$ and $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\},$ then $Y-X=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "969e2b29-61d2-5f25-8ec9-eef4bb6c965f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256652 #edb/ref 1074 "The set $Y-X$ is the set of all elements belonging to $Y$ but *not* $X.$ Note that the elements $1,3\\in X$ are also in $Y,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y-X & =\\{1,2,3,4\\}-\\{1,3,5,7\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C=\\{-3,-1,0,5,10\\}$ and $D=\\{-2,-1,0,1\\}$, then $C∖D=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d25f8686-edb7-5e4c-87dd-c8e109d70794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256677 #edb/ref 1074 "The set $C∖D$ is the set of all elements belonging to $C$ but *not* $D.$ Note that the elements $-1,0\\in D$ are also in $C,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∖D & =C-D \\\\  & =\\{-3,-1,0,5,10\\}-\\{-2,-1,0,1\\} \\\\  & =\\{-3,5,10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260686 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the listed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260686 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260686 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e6cc4d6-7551-537f-b39b-35ad7b229cf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262258 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4c40d077-b693-5acd-b033-066f298637d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262402 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a7f67e0-6e06-5a9c-bc07-e96751790a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the straight line that passes through $P(7,-11,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,2,7⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a75f14-5c7e-5e9b-9922-914a01571a2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262704 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 7t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7-3t \\\\ -11+2t \\\\ 3+7t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3t \\\\ y=-11+2t \\\\ z=3+7t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-10}{7}=\\frac{y+8}{-4}=\\frac{z+1}{11}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2f75223-42f3-536a-93af-cb8a85108498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262707 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{7}=\\frac{y-(-8)}{-4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{11},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-8,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,-4,11⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=10+7t \\\\ y=-8-4t \\\\ z=-1+11t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265262 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0929e729-c74a-53df-b186-d608a6e127ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}10 & 25 \\\\ 12 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e54120f-ad5a-5b0e-97ad-23133523c995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265926 #edb/ref 1074 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10x+25y=15 \\\\ 12x+3y=-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=3 \\\\ 4x+y=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281793 #edb/ref 1080 "[5,8)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281793 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921862