-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315285 #edb/ref 1080 "-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d12c5f6-3b92-5441-a0fc-3952c1a70749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315374 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315374 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2be071fa-e50f-5e15-af2e-800d12bec8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315491 #edb/ref 1080 "-16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592